    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ""
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Pepperpot",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "pepperpot"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " is one of the oldest dishes in the Caribbean. Traditionally it is made with "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "cassareep ",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "cassareep"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ", a dark, thick syrup pressed from cassava root that acts as a preservative. This recipe uses treacle as a substitute; it gives a similar dark colour and bittersweet flavour."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "In Guyana, pepperpot is kept on the stove and brought to a boil daily, with fresh meat added each time. For a home cook, making it the night before and reheating it the next day produces a dish with much deeper flavour than one served immediately."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
